I'm working on a 2D bullet-hell game. The enemy update loop runs every frame (60 fps) and is causing noticeable GC stutter. Here’s the core function:

```typescript
updateEnemies(dt: number) {
  const activeEnemies = this.enemies.filter(e => e.active);
  const positions = activeEnemies.map(e => e.pos);
  const velocities = activeEnemies.map(e => e.vel);

  for (const enemy of activeEnemies) {
    const nearby = this.enemies.filter(e =>
      e !== enemy && distance(e.pos, enemy.pos) < 100
    );
    const ctx = { pos: enemy.pos, vel: enemy.vel, dt };
    enemy.update(ctx, nearby);
  }

  this.projectiles.forEach(p => {
    p.pos.x += p.vel.x * dt;
    p.pos.y += p.vel.y * dt;
  });
}
```

I need to eliminate all per-frame allocations (new arrays, objects, etc.) while keeping the same behavior. The `updateEnemies` function and any helpers it calls must not allocate memory. Please rewrite the code using scratch arrays, in-place filtering, reusable context objects, and a spatial hash grid for proximity queries (cell size 100). Also ensure the projectiles update doesn't create closures or temporary objects.

Provide the optimized version with explicit notes on why each change avoids GC pressure. This function is the hottest path in the game, so performance is critical.

